      "status_notes": "CVE-2025-12781: base64.b64decode(), standard_b64decode() and urlsafe_b64decode() always accept the '+' and '/' characters, even when altchars selects a different alphabet, which can let differently encoded data pass a check. Upstream addressed it on the main branch in https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/125346 (https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/9060b4abbe475591b6230b23c2afefeff26fcca5, a CPython 3.15 change) by warning on such input. The CVE record lists CPython before 3.13.10 as affected, but the 3.13 branch received only additional tests (https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/1be80bec7960f5ccd059e75f3dfbd45fca302947), and no CPython 3.12 release carries a change to base64.py's handling of these characters. See https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2025-12781. Checked Lib/base64.py in the python-3.12 package of every build published in the DHI Alpine 3.23 repository, 3.12.12-r0 to 3.12.16-r4, x86_64 and aarch64; both architectures agree. Every published build ships the upstream 3.12 base64.py, which has no such check, so every published build is affected."
