      "status": "affected",
      "action_statement": "No fixed release is available yet; update when Docker Hardened Images publishes one.",
      "status_notes": "CVE-2025-12781: base64.b64decode(), standard_b64decode() and urlsafe_b64decode() always accept the '+' and '/' characters, even when altchars selects a different alphabet, which can let differently encoded data pass a check. Upstream addressed it on the main branch, in issue https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/125346 and commit https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/9060b4abbe475591b6230b23c2afefeff26fcca5, by emitting a warning (a DeprecationWarning or a FutureWarning) for such input, a change listed in the CPython 3.15 What's New; the 3.13 and 3.14 commits linked from the CVE record add tests only, and no CPython 3.10 release carries the change. See https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2025-12781. Checked Lib/base64.py in every binary package built from the python-3.10 source in every build published in the DHI Debian repository, 3.10.20-0 to 3.10.22-8 (25 builds), amd64 and arm64, debug packages included: it ships in libpython-3.10-stdlib up to 3.10.20-9 and in libpython-3.10-minimal from 3.10.20-10, and no other package carries a copy. Every copy is byte-identical to Lib/base64.py at the upstream v3.10.22 tag, which has no such check, so every published build is affected."
    },
